  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0149 database is a comprehensive system for managing user accounts, subscriptions, and financial transactions. At its core is the account table, which stores information about each user, including their email address, date of creation, and a unique identifier from Stripe, a payment processing service. Each account is associated with a user, who is identified by a unique ID and has a name, email address, password, and permissions level. The user's account ID is also linked to a session, which represents an active login session and includes a unique token and expiration date. The account can also be associated with a subscription, which is stored in the subscription table and includes details such as the plan, status, and start and end dates. The subscription is linked to a Stripe subscription ID and is associated with a specific account. The account can also have multiple payments, which are stored in the payment table and include the amount, currency, and status of each transaction. The payment table is linked to the account table, and each payment is associated with a unique Stripe payment ID. The plan_feature table links specific plans to features, which are stored in the feature table and include a unique name and description. The invite table stores information about pending invitations, including the email address and account ID of the recipient, while the audit_log table tracks all changes made to the system, including the user ID, account ID, action taken, and details of the change.",
  "gold_question": "1) Background / Context\n\nGravity is a Software as a Service (SaaS) boilerplate designed to expedite the development of SaaS applications. It leverages Node.js for the backend and React for the frontend. The boilerplate offers a comprehensive framework that includes user authentication, subscription management, and AI integrations with ChatGPT and DALL-E, all supported by a REST API compatible with multiple databases. \n\n2) Objectives\n\nThe primary objective of Gravity is to provide a robust and flexible starting point for developers to build custom SaaS solutions quickly. By incorporating essential SaaS features, the platform aims to reduce development time and effort, allowing developers to focus on their application's unique aspects.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Developers: Utilize the boilerplate for developing custom SaaS applications.\n- End Users: Benefit indirectly through the applications built using Gravity, involving user authentication and personalized services.\n- Administrators: Manage user roles, access controls, and oversee the application's functionalities.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n\n- User Authentication: Supports user registration, login, and session management.\n- Subscription Management: Integrates with Stripe for handling subscription plans and payments.\n- Role-based Access Control: Provides granular access permissions based on user roles.\n- AI Integrations: Integrates ChatGPT and DALL-E for AI-enhanced functionalities within applications.\n- REST API: Offers a fast, efficient API for database interactions and system operations.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Code Quality: Emphasizes a well-documented, high-quality codebase for ease of use and maintenance.\n- Performance: Designed to efficiently support multiple databases and ensure quick response times.\n- Security: Ensures secure payment processing and data integrity across the system.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_llama31/0149.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0149\nCREATE TABLE `account` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `date_created` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `stripe_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `user` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `permissions_level` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `session` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `expiration_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `session_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `subscription` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `plan` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `start_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `end_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `stripe_subscription_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `subscription_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `payment` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `amount` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `currency` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `stripe_payment_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `payment_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `plan_feature` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `plan` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `feature_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `feature` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `invite` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `invite_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `audit_log` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `action` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `details` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `audit_log_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `audit_log_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
